    "q-94616": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94616",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5},$ where $a\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{a}_{2}{t}^{5} \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =ka{t}^{5} \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
