    "q-94627": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94627",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $D=\\{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a<0\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D,$ then  ${a}_{1}<0$ and ${a}_{2}<0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n is a matrix from $D,$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}<0.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example,  if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=-1,$ then $A$ is an element of $D$ and $k\\in R,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* in  $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
