    "q-43779": {
      "dom_id": "step-q43779",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B=\\{f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)=f(-1)\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =f(-1)+g(-1) \\\\  & =(f+g)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(1) & =kf(1) \\\\  & =kf(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $B$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
